      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, can you explain the difference between high glycemic index foods and low glycemic index foods? Especially how they impact insulin response for someone managing type 2 diabetes. Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High glycemic index (GI) foods, such as refined carbohydrates like white bread, sugary snacks, and sweetened drinks, cause a rapid increase in blood sugar levels. In contrast, low GI foods like whole grains, non-starchy vegetables, and lean proteins take longer to digest, resulting in a gradual increase in blood sugar levels. For someone managing type 2 diabetes, consuming low GI foods can help regulate insulin response, reducing the risk of high blood sugar spikes and promoting better glucose control. This can contribute to improved glycemic control and overall health management.",
