      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Constant hunger or energy crashes can be frustrating. The rapid spike and crash of blood sugar levels is often caused by foods with high fructose or simple sugar content, or refined carbohydrates. When you eat these foods, the body quickly absorbs the sugar, causing a rapid increase in insulin release to regulate blood sugar levels. This can trigger an insulin crash, leaving you feeling energized for a short-term burst (the crash) before hunger returns. Additionally, foods lacking fiber, protein, and healthy fats can also contribute to an energy crash later on, as your body struggles to stabilize blood sugar levels.",
